Sure! If you already have your data in a variable called `myItemList`, you can sort it with the helper function like this:

```javascript
// Assuming myItemList looks something like this:
const myItemList = [
  { name: 'Alpha', status: 'normal' },
  { name: 'Bravo', status: 'urgent' },
  { name: 'Charlie', status: 'normal' },
  { name: 'Delta', status: 'urgent' }
];

// Call the helper function
const sorted = prioritizeAndSort({
  items: JSON.stringify(myItemList),   // pass the array as a JSON string
  priorityStatus: 'urgent',            // items with this status go first
  ascending: true                      // remaining items sorted alphabetically ascending
});

console.log(sorted);
```

The `prioritizeAndSort` function will return a new array where all items whose `status` is `"urgent"` appear first (in the order they originally appeared), followed by the rest sorted alphabetically by their `name` property in ascending order.

